# Source for q/spi-doppler3.js: third Doppler + Hemodynamics batch (calculations, Doppler instrumentation, clinical patterns).
D="Doppler"; H="Hemodynamics"
Q=[
# --- Aliasing math ---
(D,"PRF is 5 kHz, transmitted frequency 2.5 MHz, angle 0°. What is the highest velocity that can be measured without aliasing?","About 0.77 m/s",["About 1.54 m/s","About 0.39 m/s","About 3.1 m/s"],"Nyquist = 2.5 kHz. v = (2,500 × 1540) ÷ (2 × 2,500,000) ≈ 0.77 m/s.","C"),
(D,"Imaging depth is 15 cm. What is the approximate maximum PRF?","About 5,100 Hz",["About 15,000 Hz","About 1,100 Hz","About 77,000 Hz"],"PRF max ≈ 77,000 ÷ depth (cm) = 77,000 ÷ 15 ≈ 5,133 Hz.","C"),
(D,"With a 15 cm gate depth and a 2 MHz transducer at 0°, the maximum velocity before aliasing is about:","1.0 m/s",["4.0 m/s","0.25 m/s","2.6 m/s"],"Nyquist ≈ 2,567 Hz. v = 2,567 × 1540 ÷ (2 × 2,000,000) ≈ 0.99 m/s. This is why AS jets need CW.","C"),
(D,"Switching from a 5 MHz to a 2.5 MHz Doppler frequency, with the same PRF, makes the maximum measurable velocity:","Double",["Halve","Stay the same","Quadruple"],"Lower frequency halves the Doppler shift for each velocity, so twice the velocity fits under the Nyquist limit.","C"),
(D,"A PW Doppler signal aliases at 1.2 m/s. Moving the gate from 8 cm to 16 cm deep will make the aliasing velocity about:","0.6 m/s",["2.4 m/s","1.2 m/s","4.8 m/s"],"Doubling depth roughly halves the maximum PRF and the Nyquist limit.","C"),
(D,"The baseline is shifted fully so the whole PRF range shows flow in one direction. The maximum displayable velocity in that direction:","Doubles compared with a centered baseline",["Halves","Stays the same","Becomes infinite"],"A centered baseline splits the range between both directions.","C"),
# --- Doppler pulse characteristics ---
(D,"Compared with imaging pulses, pulsed Doppler pulses usually have:","More cycles (longer pulses) and a narrower bandwidth",["Fewer cycles and a wider bandwidth","The same length","No cycles"],"A narrow bandwidth allows more precise measurement of the frequency shift.","F"),
(D,"Longer Doppler pulses affect the sample volume by:","Making it longer along the beam (worse axial resolution of the gate)",["Making it shorter","Making it disappear","Improving range resolution"],"This is a trade-off for better frequency precision.","C"),
(D,"Doppler transducer crystals (especially for CW) are typically:","Less damped than imaging crystals",["More heavily damped","Not piezoelectric","Made of tungsten"],"Less damping gives a narrower bandwidth and better sensitivity.","F"),
(D,"In a dedicated CW Doppler transducer:","One crystal transmits continuously while another receives",["One crystal sends pulses and listens","There is no crystal","All crystals transmit only"],"The sample region is where the transmit and receive beams overlap.","F"),
(D,"Pulsed Doppler typically has higher temporal-average intensity than B-mode imaging because:","It repeatedly sends pulses along the same line at a high PRF",["It uses lower frequencies","It has no focusing","It uses CW"],"This is why Doppler dwell time should be limited (ALARA).","F"),
(D,"Triplex imaging (2D + color + spectral Doppler simultaneously) results in:","Lower frame rate and lower PRF for each mode",["Higher frame rate","No aliasing","Better axial resolution"],"Time is shared between three modes.","F"),
(D,"Audio Doppler output allows the sonographer to:","Hear flow characteristics, with direction often sent to left or right speakers",["Measure the angle","Measure depth","Remove aliasing"],"High-pitched sounds mean high Doppler shifts; turbulence sounds harsh.","F"),
(D,"Higher Doppler frequencies are more sensitive to slow flow in superficial vessels because:","Rayleigh scattering from red cells increases strongly with frequency",["They penetrate deeper","They alias less","They have lower attenuation"],"At depth, attenuation of high frequencies cancels this advantage.","C"),
# --- Spectral analysis ---
(D,"The spectral Doppler display's vertical axis can show either:","Doppler shift frequency (kHz) or velocity (cm/s or m/s)",["Depth or time","Intensity or power","Frame rate or PRF"],"Velocity display requires the machine to use the angle correction.","F"),
(D,"Fast Fourier transform analysis is performed:","Continuously on short segments of the Doppler signal, producing many spectra per second",["Only once per exam","Only for color Doppler","Only for B-mode"],"Each vertical line of the spectrum is one FFT result.","C"),
(D,"Spectral Doppler grayscale (brightness) is adjusted with:","Doppler gain and the spectral dynamic range or compression",["Output power only","Depth","Frame rate"],"These change display, not the measured velocities.","F"),
(D,"An 'envelope' tracing in spectral Doppler follows:","The outer edge (peak velocities) of the spectrum over time",["The baseline","The modal velocity","Noise"],"Automatic tracing can be fooled by noise; check and correct it manually.","F"),
(D,"Pulsatility index (PI) is useful because it:","Does not depend on the Doppler angle",["Depends strongly on angle","Measures absolute velocity","Requires CW Doppler"],"It is a ratio of velocities, so angle errors cancel.","C"),
(D,"The resistive index is 0.5 for a vessel with peak systolic velocity 80 cm/s. What is the end-diastolic velocity?","40 cm/s",["20 cm/s","60 cm/s","80 cm/s"],"RI = (PSV − EDV) ÷ PSV. 0.5 = (80 − EDV) ÷ 80, so EDV = 40 cm/s.","C"),
# --- Color specifics ---
(D,"The number of pulses per color scan line (packet or ensemble) is typically about:","8 to 16",["1","100 to 200","1,000"],"More pulses improve accuracy but lower the frame rate.","C"),
(D,"Color Doppler pixels show velocity as a mean because autocorrelation:","Compares the phase change between successive pulses at each location",["Calculates the full spectrum","Measures only amplitude","Uses CW"],"This is fast enough to fill an image but gives no peak velocity.","C"),
(D,"In color Doppler, increasing the color frequency (on a broadband probe) will:","Increase sensitivity to slow flow but reduce penetration and increase aliasing",["Increase penetration","Reduce aliasing","Have no effect"],"Use lower color frequencies for deep, fast flow.","C"),
(D,"A 'color sector steer' in a phased array refers to:","Angling the color box to improve the Doppler angle within the image",["Changing the frame rate only","Moving the B-mode focus","Inverting colors"],"With linear arrays, steering changes the beam angle for parallel vessels.","F"),
(D,"Color Doppler can miss a small, brief jet if:","The frame rate is too low to capture it",["The frame rate is high","The color box is small","Gain is optimal"],"Use color M-mode or narrow the box to increase temporal resolution.","F"),
(D,"Color jet area is increased by all of these EXCEPT:","Raising the Nyquist limit",["Raising color gain","Lowering the Nyquist limit","Lowering the wall filter"],"Raising the scale removes slower flow from the color map, making jets look smaller.","C"),
(D,"Variance on a color map is displayed by:","Adding green or yellow to the red or blue",["Making the color darker","Removing color","Showing black"],"High variance indicates turbulence.","F"),
# --- Clinical Doppler patterns ---
(D,"Doppler of the left ventricular outflow tract normally shows flow:","Away from the transducer in systole from the apical view",["Toward the transducer in systole","Only in diastole","Absent"],"The LVOT signal is below the baseline from apical windows.","F"),
(D,"From the apical view, mitral inflow is displayed:","Above the baseline in diastole",["Below the baseline in systole","Below the baseline in diastole","Only in systole"],"Flow moves from LA to LV, toward the apical transducer.","F"),
(D,"From the apical view, an MR jet is displayed:","Below the baseline in systole",["Above the baseline in diastole","Above the baseline in systole","Not at all"],"The jet moves from LV to LA, away from the transducer.","F"),
(D,"From the apical view, an AR jet is displayed:","Above the baseline in diastole",["Below the baseline in systole","Below the baseline in diastole","Only with PW"],"Regurgitant blood flows from aorta back into the LV toward the apex.","F"),
(D,"Pulmonary vein flow from the apical view is displayed mostly:","Above the baseline (toward the transducer), with a small Ar wave below",["Below the baseline only","Only in systole","Absent"],"The Ar reversal flows back into the vein, away from the transducer.","F"),
(D,"Hepatic vein flow from the subcostal view normally shows:","S and D waves below the baseline (toward the heart, away from transducer)",["Only flow above the baseline","Continuous flat flow","No flow"],"Reversal above the baseline in systole suggests severe TR.","C"),
(D,"Tissue Doppler of the mitral annulus normally shows:","s′ above the baseline in systole and e′ and a′ below the baseline in diastole (apical view)",["Only one wave","Waves only in systole","Waves at 1 m/s"],"Myocardial velocities are about 5 to 20 cm/s.","F"),
(D,"Color M-mode of LV inflow measures flow propagation velocity as:","The slope of the first aliasing line from the mitral valve toward the apex",["The height of the E wave","The width of the jet","The frame rate"],"A slow slope (Vp under about 50 cm/s) suggests impaired relaxation.","C"),
(D,"In a high-velocity jet recorded with CW, a 'dense' signal compared with forward flow suggests:","A large regurgitant volume",["A small regurgitant volume","Aliasing","Noise"],"Signal density reflects how many red cells are moving at those velocities.","F"),
(D,"A faint, incomplete CW signal leads to:","Underestimation of peak velocity and gradient",["Overestimation","No error","Exact values"],"Optimize alignment and gain, or use contrast enhancement.","F"),
# --- Angle effects ---
(D,"True blood velocity is 100 cm/s. At 60° with no angle correction, the displayed velocity is about:","50 cm/s",["100 cm/s","87 cm/s","200 cm/s"],"Displayed velocity = true velocity × cos 60° = 50 cm/s.","F"),
(D,"The angle correction cursor is set at 60°, but the true angle is 70°. The calculated velocity will be:","Too low, underestimating true velocity",["Too high","Exact","Zero"],"The machine divides by cos 60° (0.5) when it should divide by cos 70° (0.34), so the result is too small.","C"),
(D,"Why do echo labs usually avoid angle correction for cardiac jets?","The 3D jet direction is uncertain, so correction can introduce large errors",["Angle correction is not allowed by law","It increases aliasing","It lowers frame rate"],"Align the beam with the jet as closely as possible instead.","F"),
(D,"When the beam is perpendicular to a vessel, color Doppler shows:","Little or no color, even though blood is flowing",["Bright color","Aliasing","Mosaic"],"Tilt the transducer or steer the color box.","F"),
# --- More Doppler artifacts ---
(D,"Range ambiguity in high-PRF Doppler means:","The signal could come from more than one gate depth",["Aliasing has become worse","The velocity is overestimated","There is no signal"],"Use 2D and color to decide which gate is in the flow of interest.","F"),
(D,"Crosstalk in Doppler refers to:","A strong signal producing a mirror image on the other side of the baseline",["Two sonographers talking","Range ambiguity","Aliasing"],"Reduce gain and improve angle.","F"),
(D,"A Doppler signal from a vessel next to the one being sampled is called:","Signal contamination from an adjacent vessel (a sample volume too large or poorly placed)",["Aliasing","Mirror image","Ghosting only"],"Use a smaller gate and confirm placement with color.","F"),
(D,"Spectral 'noise' filling the whole display is reduced by:","Lowering Doppler gain",["Raising Doppler gain","Raising output to maximum","Lowering the wall filter only"],"Too much gain fills the display and hides the true envelope.","F"),
# --- Hemodynamics ---
(H,"A traditional (CGS) unit of viscosity is the:","Poise",["Rayl","Hertz","Decibel"],"Blood viscosity is a few times that of water and changes with hematocrit.","C"),
(H,"Poiseuille's law strictly applies to flow that is:","Steady, laminar and in a long, straight, rigid tube with a Newtonian fluid",["Pulsatile and turbulent","Only in veins","Only in the heart"],"Real blood flow only approximates these conditions.","C"),
(H,"A 'velocity ratio' (PSV at a stenosis ÷ PSV just before it) of more than 2 suggests:","A stenosis of at least about 50% diameter",["A normal vessel","Total occlusion","Venous obstruction"],"Velocity ratios correct for differences in overall flow and cardiac output.","C"),
(H,"Post-stenotic turbulence is usually found:","Within about 1 to 2 cm beyond a significant stenosis",["Far upstream","Only inside the narrowing","Only in veins"],"Its presence helps confirm a hemodynamically significant narrowing.","C"),
(H,"Near the entrance of a vessel, flow is usually plug-like and becomes parabolic further along. This is called:","The entrance effect",["Pressure recovery","Aliasing","Coanda effect"],"Laminar profiles develop over a distance from the entrance.","C"),
(H,"Pressure drop across a stenosis increases with velocity:","Squared",["Linearly","Not at all","To the fourth power"],"This is the basis of the simplified Bernoulli equation.","F"),
(H,"Peak velocity across a stenosis is 3.5 m/s and proximal velocity 1.0 m/s. Using the expanded Bernoulli equation, the gradient is:","45 mmHg",["49 mmHg","4 mmHg","36 mmHg"],"4 × (3.5² − 1.0²) = 4 × (12.25 − 1) = 45 mmHg.","C"),
(H,"In the arms and in the SVC, venous flow during inspiration:","Increases toward the heart",["Decreases","Stops","Reverses"],"Falling chest pressure draws blood in. Leg veins behave the opposite way because abdominal pressure rises.","C"),
(H,"In a supine patient, hydrostatic pressure differences between the head and feet are:","Minimal, because the body is at about the same level as the heart",["Very large","The same as standing","Negative everywhere"],"Standing creates large hydrostatic differences.","F"),
(H,"The jugular venous 'a' wave is caused by:","Right atrial contraction",["Tricuspid closure","Ventricular filling","Aortic valve closure"],"Large 'cannon' a waves occur when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, such as in heart block.","C"),
(H,"Flow rate units are typically:","mL/s or L/min",["cm/s","mmHg","Hz"],"Velocity is cm/s or m/s; flow rate is volume per time.","F"),
(H,"Inertia in blood flow refers to:","The tendency of blood to resist changes in velocity",["Friction with the wall","Gravity","Viscosity only"],"Accelerating blood requires extra pressure, part of the energy loss at stenoses.","C"),
(H,"A Newtonian fluid has:","Viscosity that does not change with flow rate",["Viscosity that changes with flow rate","No viscosity","Only turbulent flow"],"Blood is non-Newtonian, but behaves close to Newtonian in large arteries.","C"),
(H,"Total cross-sectional area is largest in the:","Capillaries",["Aorta","Vena cava","Pulmonary artery"],"That is why flow velocity there is slowest, allowing exchange.","F"),
(H,"Most of the pressure drop in the systemic circulation occurs across the:","Arterioles",["Aorta","Capillaries","Venules"],"Arterioles are the main resistance vessels controlling blood pressure.","F"),
(H,"A stenosis that causes no pressure drop or flow reduction at rest may become significant during exercise because:","Flow increases, raising velocity and energy losses across the narrowing",["Flow decreases with exercise","Resistance falls everywhere","The stenosis disappears"],"This is why stress testing unmasks hidden disease.","F"),
(H,"Flow in a vessel doubles while area stays the same. Mean velocity:","Doubles",["Halves","Stays the same","Quadruples"],"Velocity = flow ÷ area.","F"),
(H,"The Coanda effect describes:","A jet attaching to and following an adjacent wall",["Aliasing","Pressure recovery","Turbulence downstream"],"Wall-hugging MR jets look smaller on color than they really are.","F"),
(H,"Kinetic energy is greatest at:","The narrowest point of a stenosis, where velocity is highest",["Upstream of the stenosis","Far downstream","In the capillaries"],"Pressure energy is lowest there.","F"),
(H,"Pressure recovery is greatest when:","The aorta beyond a stenotic valve is small",["The aorta is very large","There is no stenosis","Flow is very low"],"In a small aorta, flow re-expands smoothly, regaining pressure; Doppler may overestimate the net gradient.","C"),
(H,"Reynolds number increases when:","Velocity or vessel diameter increases, or viscosity decreases",["Viscosity increases","Velocity decreases","Diameter decreases"],"Higher Reynolds numbers mean turbulence is more likely.","F"),
(H,"Venous return to the heart increases with:","Inspiration, muscle pumping and lying down",["Standing still","Valsalva strain","Dehydration"],"More venous return means more preload.","F"),
(H,"An arterial waveform with a slow upstroke and a rounded, low peak downstream of a stenosis is described as:","Tardus parvus",["Pulsus paradoxus","Pulsus alternans","Triphasic"],"Tardus means slow; parvus means small.","F"),
(H,"Pulsus alternans (alternating strong and weak beats) is a sign of:","Severe LV systolic dysfunction",["Tamponade","Normal heart","Aortic stenosis only"],"Doppler velocities alternate from beat to beat.","C"),
(H,"A bisferiens (double-peaked) pulse is associated with:","Combined aortic stenosis and regurgitation, or HCM",["Mitral stenosis","Tamponade","Pulmonary embolism"],"Two systolic peaks reflect rapid ejection followed by a second wave.","C"),
]
